y rate of $1.29 per ounce, which I can use to determine the physical silver quantities. Then I'll adjust these quantities by historical silver prices, though I still need to locate those precise historical price data points.\n\nI'll carefully convert the ounces to millions of ounces and metric tons to understand the Treasury's physical silver holdings in more tangible units of measurement.\n\nThe calculation requires three key steps: extracting the physical silver quantity, finding accurate silver prices, and then performing the inflation adjustment calculation to complete the analysis.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_b114e5f8c20b912a","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -E \"1\\.29|1\\.294|\\$1\\.29\" /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_01.txt | head -10"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
